# Problem
I track my projects in Obsidian.
But I need to create folders manually for the projects, and making it follow certain standards is not very easy.
This tool will take care of implementing a new standard for my projects that I track and work on in Obsidian in markdown files. The focus is the folder structure, the contents of the files are not of concern here.

## How the folder structure looks like
PARENT_DIRECTORY - configurable
We use indices (f"{i} - " prefix) before folder names to enforce order in the Obsidian file explorer

>PARENT_DIRECTORY
-> 1 - Finished
--> 1 - MyFirstProject
---> 1 - Subfolder
----> ... going deeper at flexible amounts for subfolders
-----> File.md (no index)
-> 2 - Current
--> 1 - MyFirstProject
---> 1 - Subfolder (means part of Subfolder, later when moves to the Finished, the contents will be merged - path-to-leaf together with filename should be unique though)
----> SomeContent.md

After running `obsidian_project_manager project1 finish '1 - MyFirstProject/1 - Subfolder'`, the file tree will look like this:
>PARENT_DIRECTORY
-> 1 - Finished
--> 1 - MyFirstProject
---> 1 - Subfolder
----> ... going deeper at flexible amounts for subfolders
-----> File.md (no index)
----> SomeContent.md
-> 2 - Current (empty)

# Tech stack

1. Use modern version of python
2. Use argparse
3. Use uv definition for the project, call it `obsidian_project_manager`. Set up hatch build-tool, make executable script to install as uv tool.
4. Provide the folder structure where each file should be
5. Create classes to create nice and clean abstractions. Use descriptive method names and explanation comments.
6. Use type hints where possible
7. Create separate files for the project as you find best fit.

# CLI interface
This tool will be used from CLI.

## Concepts
### Projects
All project commands are executed by specifying for which project is this done:
`obsidian_project_manager project <project_alias>`, which is configured through `obsidian_project_manager configure projects` as discussed later. With this information, we know where the PARENT_DIRECTORY is located, and the names of the finished and current folder names (with defaults as shown above).

So `obsidian_project_manager project <project_alias>` commands can be run from any working directory, the project path will be taken from the config, not the current working directory.

### Project Structure
The internal representation of the project has no indices in the folder names.
So for every project command, the first thing we do is read the whole directory tree of the project, use the indices to sort subfolders for each folder,
remove the indices from the names to work with the names directly.
This applies also to the user specified paths, they might or might not have indices, ignore all indices, even if incorrect.
Then at the end of the command, we compile this folder structure into the final folder names with the right indices. Rename if the name has changed, don't make unnecessary OS calls.

### Interface

#### Config
1. `obsidian_project_manager configure projects list`
Lists the current projects with their folder names

2. `obsidian_project_manager configure projects add`
Interactively asks for the details to register a new project. Saves in the config folder (working for both unix at ~/.config/... and windows)

3. `obsidian_project_manager configure projects delete <project_alias>`

#### Projects
1. `obsidian_project_manager project <project_alias> add Project/Subproject/.../FinalFolder`
Add new folder structure in `Current` folder of the project.
In one given folder there can be only one subfolder with any given index.
All subfolders should have indices.
When adding new subfolders, they should have incrementally added indices.
If the folder already exists, recursively go "inside" it instead of creating a new one.

Ignore any index prefixes provided by the user.
If the user has entered the path as `1 - Project/4 - Subproject/...`, first remove all these indices of format f"{index} - ", then only start the processing.

While going through the folders, if you find any subfolders that don't have indices specified, ask the user for confirmation (Yes/No (these one by one)/All (this approving current and all future of the session)) and rename them to add indices.

When creating the leaf folder, add an "Entry.md" file there to add to git.

2. `obsidian_project_manager project <project_alias> finish Project/Subproject/.../FinalFolder`
Move this folder structure from the Current to Finished folder.
Ignore the indices of the "incoming" (from Current), do from scratch. Indices of course apply only to folders, not files.
If there are folder name matches (ignoring the indices) between the incoming and finished folders, merge the contents.
If there are **file** name matches, fail. Do dry run before moving anything to make sure this issue won't happen after moving some files.
The indices of the subfolders across the project should always be continuously increasing. In case we are moving a subfolder into another state (here to finished), and it's creating a gap in the index flow, decrease the indices of the next subfolders to fix that.
e.g. if subfolders are 1 - first, 2 - second, 3 - third, and we finish the `2 - second`, the indices should automatically refresh to `1 - finish`, `2 - third`.
If during moves any folder becomes empty, it should be removed (recursively go up during the checks).

2. `obsidian_project_manager project <project_alias> continue Project/Subproject/.../FinalFolder`
Move this from finished to current.
Ignore indices.
If folder name conflicts arise, merge, same as in `finish`.

3. `obsidian_project_manager project <project_alias> sync`
Fix the indices of the project if anything is missing or wrong.
Alarm in case there are path+filename (ignoring indices) conflicts between finished and current folders.
Alarm in case there are path+foldername (ignoring indices) conflicts within one project state (finished or current). Meaning, in same folder there can't be multiple subfolders with same name but different indices.
Alarm in case there are path+filename (ignoring indices) conflicts within one folder. Meaning there can't be multiple files in any folder with same name but different indices.

4. `obsidian_project_manager project <project_alias> change_index {Current,Finished}/Project/Subproject/.../FinalFolder new_index`
As every subfolder has a unique index (starting with 1) in that folder, we might want to change that index to change ordering.
First we should verify if the index is out of bounds or not (e.g. if there are 3 folders, the index can't be 4, or 0, or negative).

Gaps should be filled through decreasing the higher folders indices, starting from the gap+1, until highest index.
The index conflicts should be resolved through increase of the indices starting from the conflicted, until the highest index.

As same folder-structure might be present both in current and in finished, we need to specify the stage as well in the path.